{{/*
  IMPORTANT: chezmoi matches these patterns against the *target* path
  (relative to $HOME), NOT the source path. So the entry for
  dot_local/bin/executable_claude-session must be written as
  .local/bin/claude-session — source-path spellings silently match nothing,
  which would quietly ship the excluded scripts everywhere.
  Verify with: chezmoi ignored

  Keyed on .role (set per machine in ~/.config/chezmoi/chezmoi.toml by the
  bootstrap scripts), defaulting to "restricted" in .chezmoidata.yaml.
*/}}

{{/*
  What "no Claude Code on the work laptop" actually means: the CLI is never
  INSTALLED there, and nothing on that machine ever executes it. It does NOT
  mean the machine can't reach the session running on the server.

  So the line this file draws is "does the file run claude locally?", not
  "does the file have claude in its name":

    claude-attach / claude-env   they run a herdr CLIENT locally (or ssh/mosh
                                 to one on the server) and `claude` never runs
                                 here at all — the client only draws terminals
                                 the SERVER owns. Ship everywhere; this is how
                                 a restricted client reaches the session.
    claude-vscode                same idea: runs the local `code` CLI (VS Code
                                 itself is fine on restricted clients) plus
                                 claude-attach. Ships everywhere too.
    70_claude.sh                 the ca/ce/cv aliases for the above. Same.
    claude-session               runs `claude` in the current shell. Server only.
                                 Now reached only through the disabled
                                 claude-tmux.service fallback, which is why it
                                 still ships rather than being retired.
    claude-open                  starts a herdr workspace running `claude` here.
                                 Server only, same rule.
    codex-open                   the same for `codex`, and additionally
                                 attaches it to the app-server daemon, which
                                 only exists on the server. Server only.
    codex-sessions               lists and deletes saved Codex conversations by
                                 reading ~/.codex and running `codex delete`.
                                 Server only, where those sessions live.
    herdr-main-workspace         starts `claude` in the claude-main herdr pane,
                                 over the socket API. Server only, same rule.
    claude-telegram-bot          long-polls Telegram and calls claude-open.
                                 Server only — it is the phone entry point to
                                 the machine that actually runs the CLI.

  Enforcement of the never-install rule lives where an install would actually
  happen: no CLI install step in client-work-bootstrap.sh (which verifies the
  absence afterwards), autoInstallIdeExtension:false in the managed VS Code
  settings, and 70_work_guard.sh below.
*/}}
{{- $claudeInstalled := eq .role "server" }}

{{- if not $claudeInstalled }}
{{/*
  Runs claude in-process, so it only belongs where the CLI exists.
*/}}
.local/bin/claude-session
.local/bin/claude-open
.local/bin/codex-open
.local/bin/codex-sessions
.local/bin/herdr-main-workspace
{{/*
  cx / cxo run codex in the current shell, so they follow codex-open exactly.
*/}}
.config/shell/source/70_codex.sh
{{/*
  Starts sessions via claude-open and codex-open, and its systemd unit only
  exists on the server. Shipping it elsewhere would put a bot token's worth of
  attack surface on a laptop for no gain.
*/}}
.local/bin/claude-telegram-bot
{{/*
  The CLAUDE.md priming note for the dev log and the vault only makes sense
  where Claude Code itself runs.

  The tooling it refers to is NOT listed here, because chezmoi no longer ships
  it: /log-session, the second-brain skill, dev-log-entry and vault-note live
  in the second-brain repo under .tools/ and are symlinked into place by
  link_vault_tooling (provision/lib/repos.sh), which only the server bootstrap
  calls. That call is their role gate now.
*/}}
.claude/CLAUDE.md
{{/*
  Blog writing flow. /post-ideas and /post-brief read ~/second-brain directly and are
  the private half of a split described in dot_zshenv — the public half
  (/post-scaffold, /post-ship) lives in the warroyo-blog repo and is not managed
  here at all. Same rule as the writer above: they only make sense where Claude
  Code runs.

  DEV_LOG_PITCHES needs no entry here. It lives in .zshenv, which has to ship
  everywhere, and self-gates on ~/second-brain existing. That directory is now
  cloned on every machine (provision/lib/repos.sh) — the vault itself is not
  role-gated, only the commands above that write to it — so the variable is set
  everywhere and simply unused where those commands do not ship.
*/}}
.claude/commands/post-ideas.md
.claude/commands/post-brief.md
{{/*
  Backlog: the backlog skill and the backlog-add / backlog-list /
  backlog-subtask scripts it funnels through. Same rule again — a skill is only any use where Claude Code runs.
  The whole skills directory is ignored, not just backlog/, so a restricted
  client does not grow an empty ~/.claude/skills.
*/}}
.local/bin/backlog-add
.local/bin/backlog-list
.local/bin/backlog-subtask
.claude/skills
.claude/skills/**
{{- end }}

{{- if or (eq .role "server") (eq .role "personal") }}
{{/* The VS Code auto-install guard is pointless on the machines that are
     allowed to have Claude Code; it exists for the restricted clients. */}}
.config/shell/source/70_work_guard.sh
{{- end }}

{{- if ne .role "personal" }}
{{/*
  paste-image is macOS-only (it shells out to osascript to read the clipboard)
  and exists to push an image to the server, so it only makes sense on the
  personal Air. Excluding it elsewhere keeps a broken script off the server.
*/}}
.local/bin/paste-image
{{- end }}

{{- if ne .role "server" }}
{{/* Headless-server-only: remap $BROWSER to print URLs rather than launch one. */}}
.config/shell/source/60_browser.sh
{{- end }}

{{- if ne .chezmoi.os "linux" }}
{{/*
  macOS gets its ssh-agent from launchd, and AddKeysToAgent/UseKeychain in
  ~/.ssh/config handle key loading there, so none of this is needed.
*/}}
.config/shell/source/60_ssh_agent.sh
{{- end }}
